                    text: "**Azad** has 1,700+ qualified parts, each taking 30–48 months to approve, and CARE names only four makers worldwide for similar parts; Mitsubishi gave it an eight-year single-source contract for hot-section vanes it used to make itself, and Azad's margin has risen from 18–19% years ago to 36–39%. **Raymond**'s aerospace arm has 350+ LEAP engine parts and took ten years to climb from the least to the second-most critical parts ('It took us 5 years to go from N4 to N3'). **Aequs** has 5,740 approved parts and won the first complete A320 wheel ever assembled outside Safran's own plants, but it deliberately keeps margins moderate: 'If we increase margins too much, our win rate globally might come down.' **Sansera** says it is the only Indian firm machining an aero-engine blisk, and its chip-equipment customer is 'blocking machining capacity'. **My view: these approvals last for the life of an engine, but because the customer owns the drawing, they protect volume more than price.**",
