    closing: {
        heading: '9. Summary review',
        body: [
            "**Global aircraft supply chain.** **Sansera: follow.** The best executor, raising guidance, but still four-fifths auto and priced ahead of my estimate. **Raymond: follow.** A 22-year engine qualification inside an auto-parts holding company; watch the cash and the warrants. **Unimech: wait** for serial parts to replace one-off tooling now that treasury income has gone. **Aequs: wait** for the consumer arm to break even and for the warrant issue to be explained. **Rossell: avoid.** Build-to-print margins, debt at 2.6 times net worth and sales routed through the promoter's tea company.",
            "**Energy turbines and fuel cells.** **MTAR: follow.** Exceptional execution, one customer, a full price even after I raise my estimate. **Azad: wait**, down from follow. The hardest qualification here, but −₹61cr of operating cash against ₹317cr of profit over FY22–FY26; it returns to follow once operating cash turns positive and covers most of its profit.",
            "**Indian defence electronics.** **Astra: follow**, moved down from own: Uttam gives real visibility, but guidance is 16–18% a year until FY29 and the price now asks for more; operating cash was ₹205cr against ₹576cr of profit over FY22–FY26, though FY26 alone brought in ₹387cr. **Data Patterns: follow.** The best design franchise, finally converting its delayed orders, but only ₹162cr of ₹893cr of FY22–FY26 profit arrived as operating cash (18%); buy on a fall once operating cash passes half of profit. **Zen: wait** until orders catch up with the ₹4,000cr target. **ideaForge: wait** for the Army's fast-track orders. **Avantel: pass**, up from avoid: orders are back, but margins and governance are not. **Digilogic: pass**, down from wait, after a missed first promise and a large family pay rise. **Apollo: avoid.** Acquisitions, dilution, subsidiary loans and a pledged promoter. **AXISCADES: pass**, up from avoid. The defence business is real and growing fast, but the price already assumes the plan works, the earnings were sold and the plan depends on sale money and deals not yet delivered. **Aartech: avoid.** Too small, and its profit comes from treasury gains.",
            "**Materials and naval parts.** **Krishna Defence: own**, and keep the position small. The best returns here from approved import substitutes, at the only price that fits; but orders are thin, and it drops to follow if the book is still below ₹150cr by December. **Mishra Dhatu Nigam (MIDHANI): pass.** A strategic asset whose protection has been withdrawn, with weak governance this year.",
            "**What would change my view.** For the better: Krishna's order book passing ₹150cr; Astra's QRSAM and Su-30 orders arriving; Data Patterns turning profit into cash; Azad's operating cash turning positive; Zen winning anti-drone and simulator tenders; ideaForge's fast-track orders landing; Aequs' consumer arm breaking even. For the worse: an AI data-centre slowdown (MTAR, Azad, Sansera, Unimech, Rossell together), further slippage in Indian order placement, another fixed-price cost shock, and more promoter warrants or pledges.",
            "**What to watch next quarter.** Krishna: tender wins and the order book. Astra: revenue recovery after Q1. Data Patterns: new orders and cash. MTAR: Phase 2 capacity and Bloom volumes. Sansera: aerospace sales and free cash. Azad: H2 ramp and working capital. Unimech: Hobel's first full quarter. Zen: orders. Aequs: consumer losses. ideaForge: new orders. Avantel, Digilogic and MIDHANI: H1 revenue and margins. Apollo: the mine order and Premier.",
        ],
        bottomLine:
            "In aerospace and defence the moat is a part number on a drawing or a development partnership with the customer, both of which bring years of re-orders without a tender, but the cash arrives slowly and the prices already know. Own Krishna Defence & Allied Industries, small; follow Astra Microwave Products, Data Patterns, MTAR Technologies, Sansera Engineering and Raymond for better prices; wait on Azad Engineering until its profit arrives as cash, and on Unimech, Zen, Aequs and ideaForge for named events; and avoid the companies whose growth is bought with acquisitions, dilution, related-party money or a forecast.",
    },

